# Example Lesson

Replace this file with real material. It exists to show the shape a
lesson takes; delete the directory once you have your own.

A lesson is one self-contained piece of teaching — roughly what a
lecturer would cover in one sitting. The pipeline reads the whole file
and writes lecture prose, study-guide sections, and retrieval chunks
from it, so the structure below is a guide rather than a schema: use
ordinary markdown headings and prose.

## What a lesson should contain

Explain the mechanism, not just the conclusion. Downstream prompts are
told never to invent facts, so anything the lecture should say has to be
here first.

Numbers, definitions, and processes are worth stating plainly — they are
extracted into the study guide, the glossary, and the flashcard deck.

## Figures

Put images in an `images/` subdirectory beside this file. PNG, JPEG,
GIF, WebP, BMP, and SVG are read; anything else is ignored. A vision
model describes each figure once and folds the description into the
prose, so a diagram with no caption still reaches the audio.

SVGs work but need `rsvg-convert` installed, and the EPUB omits them —
ship a raster copy alongside if the figure matters.

## Equations

Write equations in fenced code blocks so they survive verbatim:

```
extract yield = (litres of alcohol) / (tonnes of grain)
```

## If your material is not already in lessons

Most material is not. A book, a PDF export, or a pile of documents has
chapters or files, not lessons — and a good lesson breakdown is its own
piece of work. Use `etude ingest` to propose one from unstructured
source material and review the proposal before it becomes a course.
